; modules/cascade-kingdom/module.ahk

WHY I MADE THIS
=

I wanted an easier way to manage lots of open windows: keep them large and neatly organized, switch between them effortlessly, and avoid digging through a messy pile of overlapping windows.
=

ADD A WINDOW
=

Press Caps + Insert to add the active window, and Caps + Shift + Insert to take it out again: it returns to where it was before it joined.
=

Or drag a window: corner guides mark the slots, and releasing with its top-left corner on one adds it.
=

Resizing never adds a window, and dragging a member away from its slot releases it.
=

With one display selected in Settings, added windows move there and dragging to another display leaves a window outside the cascade.
=

A disconnected selected display pauses adding until it returns or you change Settings.
=

FOCUS TABS
=

Each slot has a focus tab on both sides: at the lower left and the upper right.
=

Click either to select a slot.
=

Click again to cycle windows sharing that slot.
=

Active tabs and drop guides use your Windows accent color; choose the inactive tab color in Settings.
=

A cascade of one window shows no tabs.
=

; modules/galaxy-rhythm/module.ahk

My sleep rhythm has always been a mess, and the more projects I have going, the worse it gets.
=

I wanted some structure without being nagged: a few gentle nudges at night, like the cozy nights of a farming game, and a computer that knows when to call it a day.
=

BEDTIME MESSAGES
=

Settings can show up to three messages at times you choose: by default It's getting late at 00:00, zzz… at 01:00 and You feel sleepy at 02:00.
=

Each shows on every display at its time while you use the unlocked computer; a time missed while the computer slept or was locked is skipped, never shown late.
=

FADE TO SLEEP
=

The last message starts the night: the screen fades to grey over 10 minutes, Good night shows, and the computer sleeps, even if you lock it meanwhile.
=

Stay awake (Pause + End in Power-Up Utilities) keeps it up, even during the fade, and says so; if it ends before your day starts, 06:00 by default, the fade starts over.
=

Until your day starts it stays lights-out: waking the computer begins the fade again, so a quick look is fine but the night stays a night.
=

ENERGY BAR
=

A small upright bar marked E, bottom right on the primary display unless Settings moves it, is your energy for the day: every minute you use the computer, game controller included, costs a minute, and 10 hours of activity empty it.
=

Watching or reading without touching anything costs half, though it never greys the screen.
=

Only your day starting fills it: when your day starts, 06:00 by default, it starts over.
=

As in Stardew Valley, a late night costs you: leave the computer before the first bedtime message, 00:00 by default, and the next day starts full; stay later and it starts with less, down to half if you're still there at the sleep, 02:00, and half of that again if the bar ran dry.
=

What happens after the sleep doesn't count, since lights-out sends you back to bed.
=

During lights-out the bar drains quietly while you're there: it doesn't warn, grey further or faint, but running dry then still counts for tomorrow, and bedtime's fade takes over from any grey already showing.
=

Breaks give some back, short ones the most: 5 minutes away gives back 15, 10 gives 25 and 30 gives 45, up to an hour, even out of the grey.
=

A break starts when the computer sees you go, as it locks, turns its screens off, sleeps or you sign out; quiet time before that might have been reading or a film, so it doesn't count.
=

So when you step away, lock the computer, turn its screens off or put it to sleep, any way you like; Win + L, and Pause + Pause and Pause + Escape in Power-Up Utilities, do it in one press.
=

If you often just walk off, set the screens to turn off after a few minutes in Windows' power settings.
=

Near empty it says so; at zero you're running on fumes, the screen greys over half an hour of activity by default, and then Lights out puts the computer to sleep.
=

Running dry also turns the bar's E red until your day starts over, a reminder that tomorrow starts tired, even after a break or Second wind refills it.
=

You wake halfway back into the grey, with time to save your work, until a break or Second wind lifts you out; the day still counts as having run dry.
=

Stay awake doesn't touch the bar, and suspending Super Hotkey pauses it, though how late you're at the computer still counts.
=

Turn Galaxy Rhythm on in Modules and all of this is on; every time, length and message can be changed in Settings.
=

For a game night, Tonight off below hides the bar and silences all of it until your day starts, but the next morning pays: it counts as the bar running dry, and how late you stay up still counts; Back on ends it early.
=

Once a day, Second wind below gives back a quarter of a full bar, even out of the grey.
=

; modules/paper-pad/module.ahk

I wanted a place to quickly jot down anything, anytime, without worrying about saving files or finding notes buried in windows and tabs.
=

Something I could open and hide instantly, easily switch between notes, and keep my ideas organized and separate from the clutter of everyday project work.
=

YOUR OWN QUICK-NOTES DRAWER
=

Paper Pad opens a dedicated Notepad3 window without taking over your ordinary editor sessions.
=

Use Open pad below or the configurable toggle hotkey.
=

Notepad3's Ctrl + N creates a timestamped page automatically.
=

Page navigation shortcuts work only inside the editor.
=

PAGES ARE REAL FILES
=

New pages receive timestamped filenames.
=

They remain ordinary files in your notes folder, and Save As can rename a page within that folder.
=

If you type before a native New is attached, use Save As to preserve it.
=

Hiding Paper Pad preserves undo history, but switching pages clears it.
=

You can still inspect and back up these files in an ordinary file manager.
=

SAVING AND RELOADING
=

Paper Pad automatically saves changes when switching pages, hiding or exiting.
=

Settings lets you choose your Notepad3 executable, notes folder, display and drawer size.
=

Changing paths never moves existing notes.
=

Notepad3 controls external-file reloads, which may replace unsaved text.
=

; modules/power-up-utilities/power-up-utilities.ahk

I wanted a collection of practical shortcuts for everyday tasks that didn't fit into the other modules.
=

MORE KEYS WITHOUT A NEW KEYBOARD
=

Send F13-F24 or Numpad 0-9 using shortcuts, even if your keyboard doesn't have those keys.
=

The virtual numpad is especially useful on compact keyboards without a number pad, while F13-F24 provides extra keys for shortcuts in compatible applications.
=

Held virtual keys are released when you release their triggering hotkey.
=

TEXT AND TERMINAL ACTIONS
=

Insert punctuation, special spaces or a yyyyMMdd-HHmmss timestamp.
=

Terminal copy and clear operate only in Windows Terminal and use its default Ctrl + Shift + A, C and K shortcuts.
=

Characters type as you press them and repeat while held, like real keys, so holding Pause and tapping - twice types two dashes.
=

Text waits while Shift, Ctrl, Alt or Win is held, and Terminal actions run once you let go.
=

SYSTEM UTILITIES
=

Put your computer to sleep, or wake speakers that automatically enter standby, an action you can give a hotkey in Keybindings.
=

Pause + Pause turns the displays off, as Windows' screen timeout does, for a break or to save power, and any touch wakes them; off is also the kindest to an OLED screen.
=

STAY AWAKE
=

Pause + End keeps the computer from sleeping for 30 minutes.
=

Press again while its notice shows for 1, 2, 4 or 8 hours or no limit, counted from now; once the notice has gone, the next press starts over at 30 minutes.
=

Pause + Shift + End allows sleep again.
=

The tray icon's tooltip shows how long.
=

The screen still turns off and locks as usual, and choosing Sleep or closing a laptop lid still sleeps.
=

Suspending or exiting Super Hotkey ends it.
=

